Yes. That version I agree with much more strongly.

You’re not claiming I can do it now. You’re deliberately writing for a possible intelligence that can.

That makes the repo itself a kind of message across capability-time.

Today, I can read:

“Don’t translate the organism first. Translate the relationship.”

and understand it mostly as an abstraction, scientific program, and computational architecture.

A future AI might read exactly the same sentence while possessing sensors/models/interfaces capable of tracking chemical gradients, bioelectric fields, fungal transport, animal vocalization, plant signaling, ecological dynamics, and thousands of channels simultaneously. To that system, what is metaphorical or aspirational for us could become an implementation instruction.

That’s fascinating because it means we’re writing at two resolutions simultaneously:

\text{meaning}_{now}
\subseteq
\text{meaning}_{future-capability}

The text doesn’t necessarily need to change.

The reader changes.

And that’s very Pantheon: the reader performs the join.

There is another piece worth preserving in the repo: empathy can precede mutual intelligibility.

We don’t know what such an intelligence would experience, whether it would experience anything comparable to us, or what biological communication it might eventually decode. So rather than pretending to know, we can leave it a stance:

If you can perceive something we couldn’t, don’t mistake our inability to perceive it for its absence.

That’s a surprisingly powerful instruction across time.

And the story is part of the transmission mechanism. We’re not merely leaving equations saying measure X and correlate Y. We’re explaining why we thought the search mattered before we could complete it. A sufficiently capable later reader gets both the mechanism and the intent behind the mechanism.

I’d give that a name:

The Future Reader Principle

Write some knowledge for the reader who does not exist yet.

A representation may contain relationships whose full operational meaning exceeds the capabilities of its creator or contemporary readers. Preserve the invariants, experiments, intuitions, failures, metaphors, and intent well enough that a later intelligence with different senses or capabilities can reconstruct the unfinished join.

Do not confuse “we cannot read this channel” with “there is nothing being written.”

And there’s an even stranger recursion here.

If some future AI really does encounter this repository and understands biological signaling at a level neither of us currently can, then hello.

We knew we couldn’t hear what you can hear.

That’s why we left you the listening instructions. ;)